    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7b2eeee6_e16d8fc0",
        "filename": "power/sdm845.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 580,
      "author": {
        "id": 1113904
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-16T18:11:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t like this either, especially this early in development.\n\nThe issue is that turning on the 5V rail temporary (O(1ms)) requires a lot of current (I think in my previous patches I had to go all the way up to 5A on the charger). After the 5V capacitors are charged, it probably requires very little current after that.\n\nTo make matters worse, our leds are on that rail.\n\nThere are also other rails (stuff that\u0027s required for the AP) that sometimes requires more current from the charger too.\n\nRealistically I don\u0027t think a proper type C power supply will brown out with this. I guess that\u0027s why the charger has 2 current limits (one for spikes like ours and another standard one that will probably follow the PD negotiated current). Of course, low power 500mA cables will still brown out regardless.\n\nSo without a battery, we either can\u0027t turn on the led (or usb vbus, but which isn\u0027t always needed). Or we do something hacky like this to prevent any booting unless we have a good enough power supply.",
      "parentUuid": "c0b2beaa_f99ba15f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 578,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 580,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "27a75060d8405380ae2611def93bfcb0bb0e6114",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3d94be44_3d022f26",
        "filename": "power/sdm845.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 580,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000407
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-16T20:23:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This check is for the case of no-battery and powered by low-power-adapter. For the early development, people usually uses bare boards. They may plug some unknown low-power-adapter that results a reboot loop. This check catches this issue.\n\nThe 7.5W is mainly for enabling 5V rail. I moved it to the time when power-on/off AP, which makes more sense than G3 \u003c-\u003e S5. So logic is like:\n * If the battery level is \u003e\u003d2%, this check passes and then enables 5V rail (which doesn\u0027t brown out).\n * If no battery plugged, makes sure the adapter has enough power (may wait a while for PD negotiation). If no enough power, does boot AP and transitions back to S5 (\nthe new patch uses the regular state machine).",
      "parentUuid": "7b2eeee6_e16d8fc0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 578,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 580,
